--- !u!114 &7686131481758405253
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2617597790435150115}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "\nName: Harold Auden\r\nAge: 36\r\n\nStrengths:\r\nIntelligence\r\nSkepticism\r\nCaution\r\n\r\nWeakness:\r\nFear
    of not being respected\r\nFear of disease (Hypochondriac)\r\nPhysically weak\r\nPhotosensitive\r\n\n\n\nCharacter
    Story on Next Page\n\n\tHarold Auden is a Professor of Gothic Literature at Miskatonic
    University. A man in his mid-thirties with the face of a pubescent teenager,
    he shows up to his classes in a three-piece suit, no matter the weather. Wearing
    his father\u2019s shoes, he enters the classroom freshly polished every morning.
    He wears thick black glasses that fog up in the cold winter months and carries
    his books in a leather satchel that shows its wear from years of use. A tall,
    slender man, his arms and legs look as if they could snap off from a strong breeze.
    \r\nWaking up to his phone alarm, Harold turns his lamp on, striking his eyes.
    Dr. Auden starts his days with a bowl of oatmeal with brown sugar and three blueberries.
    After which, he drives into work. Arriving at approximately seven thirty every
    morning,  he walks into a lecture hall older than him. The tube lights buzz as
    he flicks the stiff switch, turning his head down and quickly sanitizing his
    hands. He spends an hour reading in front of the empty auditorium before preparing
    for the day\u2019s classes. The classes are mainly attended by upperclassmen.
    Many worry that the decades to come could turn them into a version of Professor
    Auden.  Harold feels the collective pity. After the final lecture , he climbs
    back into the car and heads straight home, reading more before shutting the lights
    off.  \r\n\tHis love of the Gothic comes from when he was a child. Harold spent
    much of his early childhood bedridden with an immune deficiency. It left him
    watching the neighborhood kids out a windowpane from the second story of suburban
    row house. When he did get the chance to leave the house, there was always something
    to be wary of. \u201CDon\u2019t touch that.\u201D \u201CDon\u2019t go out in
    that.\u201D \u201CDon\u2019t stand so close to that.\u201D The words of his mother 
    echoed in his ears. Stuck in his room, Harold escaped into books. His librarian
    mother brought home whatever Harold asked, and often there was a recurring cast.
    Lovecraft, Jackson, King. He read as much horror as he could get, naturally drawn
    to the abject and the unspoken. \r\n\tAs Harold grew into a teen and his condition
    improved, he couldn\u2019t shake the isolation. It was familiar to him. To school
    and back, everyday. When it was time to go to college, it was obvious what he
    wanted to do and with a degree like his, there weren\u2019t options with anything
    short of a PhD. So that\u2019s what he did. But after only a year or two struggling
    to become a writer and critic, Harold boomeranged right back to Miskatonic University
    where he used his limited success to get a full-time professorship teaching Gothic
    Literature.  \r\n\tStumbling upon a dusty book during his weekend retreats to
    the college archives, he pulled the tome bound with a smooth, thick material
    off the shelf to investigate. The rubber-like material was fraying at the edges
    but the pages were as white as if it had just been printed. The last thing Dr.
    Auden remembered before waking up here was reading the first few words of the
    book: REDACTED"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 5b915f831aacd3649af76d10b953082e, type: 2}
